==Latvian==

===Etymology 1===
From an unattested verb {{m|ine-bsl-pro|*balt||to become white}} (of which {{m|lv|balts}} originally was the [[past participle]] form; compare. {{etyl|lt|-}} verb {{m|lt|bálti}}, and compare. {{etyl|lv|-}} 17th-century derived verb {{m|lv|baltīt||to make, paint something white}}, later replaced by other verbs, derived from {{m|lv|balts}}: from {{etyl|bat-pro|lv}} {{m|ine-bsl-pro|*bal-}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|lv}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰel-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*bʰol-||shiny, white}}. Cognates include {{cog|lt|báltas}}, {{etyl|xsv|-}} {{m|sv|baltas}}. In several Indo-European languages, reflexes of the stem {{m|ine-pro|*bʰel-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*bʰol-}} are often found in words relating to water or humid places, probably due to their shiny, reflective surfaces: {{cog|xil|*balta}} (“marsh, swamp”), {{cog|sq|baltë||mud, sludge, swamp}}, Proto-Slavic {{m|sla-pro|*bolto||swamp, lake}} ({{cog|cu|блато||lake|tr=blato}}, {{cog|ru|болото||marsh, swamp|tr=bolóto}} (dialectal “puddle, lake”), {{cog|cs|bláto||mud; pl. swamp}}, {{cog|pl|błoto||mud, swamp}}). This usage is also attested in Baltic languages, as in, e.g., {{etyl|prg|-}} placename {{m|prg|Namuynbalt}} (swamp). It left also traces in Latvian, in the names of lakes or swamps (''Baltenis'', ''Baltiņa purvs''), and is a possible source of the word {{m|lv|balti||Balts, Baltic}}.<ref name="LEV"/>

